a80e24757d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522197} {:answer/id #uuid "9caaa3b1-2fd7-5093-989a-4a1ad46a3f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522339} {:answer/id #uuid "a998a8d9-6a09-5d04-acb0-872ca47788d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522375} {:answer/id #uuid "fed8e2de-30fd-518a-ac16-176c89ef2fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522162} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b1e7ca-994b-5508-b638-3a8f99c45170" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e119a1c3-3fb4-5a0d-b097-eb9b2cba2dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338911" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n5B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n10\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{20}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2a58e732-9b94-5f3d-88a9-7a4d9823485f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18495168-a2f2-5760-9658-64137996b12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522072} {:answer/id #uuid "38ad86d5-fe25-58e4-90bc-5eac3ba443d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{18}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522141} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d43ac0-9269-5f2e-ac1f-07ceda1132ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{18}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522369} {:answer/id #uuid "db16e7ff-5f98-57eb-80cb-369c7c0fc1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522369} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5d72b2-1ac6-56f0-b377-5ca377210957" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "619c5499-6599-5620-83f9-56cce4f5fab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522221} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d1ea79-2eda-572c-80ad-221ef3734d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{-x/3}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522351} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bbc9f2-f65d-50b3-8c20-aa6c05a8948c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522382} {:answer/id #uuid "e537a154-19fd-52b4-8885-cfa1f1c64a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522382} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cec229b-c780-5301-8ffe-447f6b02d795" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d3e4d429-39a4-5565-a491-a0dbab9cb6bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338898" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions.\n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+6{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{12\\lambda }+2B{e}^{12\\lambda }+6(6A\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }+B{e}^{12\\lambda }+12B\\lambda {e}^{12\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+6(6A\\lambda +B+12B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+36A\\lambda +6B+72B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+8(1+9\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+36\\lambda )A+(8+72\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1+6\\lambda  \\\\ 1+36\\lambda  & 8+72\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (8+72\\lambda )-(1+6\\lambda )(1+36\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 48\\lambda +432{\\lambda }^{2}-(1+42\\lambda +216{\\lambda }^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 216{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda +1)(18\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}B=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=B,$ where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =B{e}^{-x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{18},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+(1+6\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}A+\\frac{4}{3}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-4B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-4B{e}^{x/3}+Bx{e}^{x/3} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/3}(x-4) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/3}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x/2}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/3}(x-4).$"} :step/id #uuid "4affdd5b-9eb0-5e02-a8fc-15da98d24def" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567fc8bd-0545-5514-bad2-2530be47b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0f9caa-c235-5c33-97a8-ed37267ce102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312970} {:answer/id #uuid "8a976a0d-04cc-5469-b5d5-ce40485a3111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313076} {:answer/id #uuid "d4033229-d039-5591-9725-ef504b3bf910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312673} :answer-field/id #uuid "9766fe16-4357-5c95-855f-d195ff0899f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0c1d35-619b-50f3-8ba4-8bf44b8735d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} {:answer/id #uuid "33b15917-a630-5540-8ff2-3c7afd154552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312391} {:answer/id #uuid "dff83fa5-d810-5b3d-93f2-da5aeaca018d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313737} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a4fe9-3896-59ae-b9c8-70c510922d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312166} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6ef2dc-9acf-53d3-a7cb-4c48b67ab3c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b03b233d-646c-56b8-aed5-c92855708d24" :question/math-academy-id "q-338921" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "843f985f-228b-50fa-8fb3-bdfb81168ccb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463598a2-802d-5891-9146-cf86fa6b8eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+4n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522171} {:answer/id #uuid "568b0e44-93e7-5f72-ae24-3f002855e7b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522201} {:answer/id #uuid "5f820d1b-c322-595d-b365-4644c6d4d4b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522218} {:answer/id #uuid "f768356d-54ca-54ba-94ac-a515ad1c7618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522545}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522218} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a117e57-3697-5755-a2e7-a974d6fc679a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4dd299-25b5-5ac9-8cdd-da41260030e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522079} {:answer/id #uuid "825a9c42-aa52-56eb-a2d0-d8428c2fac85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522280} {:answer/id #uuid "ba16303c-d7ba-5f9a-8c48-d91e998775c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522404} {:answer/id #uuid "fa306cfd-ae11-5cd7-a248-0203ef2f5d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522280} :answer-field/id #uuid "af81751a-f88f-51b1-958a-22d5515c3642" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "733b1be5-001a-56d5-87cc-51209a854a00" :question/math-academy-id "q-338712" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$  {{field-1}}, where $n\\in {N}_{0}.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not a parameter of the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n2B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{\\lambda }=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(1+2n)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in {N}_{0}.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(1+2n)^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9413bf48-d935-5135-a6f0-348e7d65f04d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189bdb17-0255-56a4-88ef-9dcc495a2f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{16}$ and $\\frac{1}{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312147} {:answer/id #uuid "2d29855b-7d03-5d40-a241-8245ed020564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{8}$ and $\\frac{1}{16}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312328} {:answer/id #uuid "86cc67e1-0dc1-513d-a57e-eb1363728f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313054} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2334cb-653c-5f19-b972-baf5be78ab08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312328} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e55d5e5-b6ad-5f91-a5da-b2832166aad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bc3189-1d45-5e38-b2ab-7948fb50c3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311980} {:answer/id #uuid "0beb7f9f-5037-534f-a005-ded2c3063827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312063} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dce20f-3eff-52f1-8812-ba2c6da7d13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814} {:answer/id #uuid "f150ddf8-b85e-5c47-a8bb-b1cba29e7bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb08e2d-d624-5c25-9c0b-d5ba38fba6a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b441f0c-d5e0-5b6a-9f79-45716a07debc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338900" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "b7a36c8c-b03f-5f19-ac0b-d15bd0a7bd0a" 